-- title:
--   Ramification of X₀(N) over the j-line, intrinsic form
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field, $N\ge 1$ with $N\ne 0$ in $K$, and $j_0\in K$. Let $M$ be a field extension of $K$ with decidable equality, $t\in M$ transcendental over $K$ and $M$ finite over $K(t)$. Let $W_0$ be a place of $M$ over $K$ in the project's sense — a valuation subring $A\subsetneq M$ containing $K$ and a principal ideal ring — with decidable equality on its residue field $\kappa$, and assume $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0}(t-j_0)>0$. Let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $A$ whose reduction $\widetilde W=W\otimes_A\kappa$ and whose generic fibre $W_M$ both have nonzero discriminant, with $j(W_M)=t$. Let $Q\in W_M$ have exact additive order $N$, assume Vélu's full kernel quotient $W_M/\langle Q\rangle$ (built from the sums of `veluGx`, `veluGy` over the odd-order summing set of $Q$) has nonzero discriminant, and let $\psi$ be a $K$-algebra map from $K(j(q^d):d\mid N)\subseteq K((q))$ to $M$ with $\psi(j(q))=t$ and $\psi(j(q^N))=j(W_M/\langle Q\rangle)$. Then $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0}(t-j_0)$ times the number of variable changes over $\kappa$ fixing $\widetilde W$ and carrying the cyclic group generated by the reduction of $Q$ into itself (via `Point.vcInvFun`) equals the ramification index of $W_0$ along $\psi$ — the least $n>0$ with $\operatorname{ord}_{W_0}(\psi f)=n$ for some $f\ne 0$ — times the number of variable changes over $\kappa$ fixing $\widetilde W$.
--
--   This is the modular interpretation of the ramification of $X_0(N)\to X(1)$ at a pair $(E_0,C_0)$, expressed for an arbitrary realisation of the pair over a finite extension of the $j$-line with a good model at a place above $j_0$, rather than for a specially constructed Galois model. In this intrinsic shape it can be applied both to $(W_M,\langle Q\rangle)$ and to the isogenous datum $(W_M/\langle Q\rangle, W_M[N]/\langle Q\rangle)$, and it is used in the computation of ramification indices and degrees for the level structures occurring later in the argument.
